Long COVID Neurocognitive Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-viral brain fog and memory deficit

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-026
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute confusion, severe word-finding difficulty, and total cognitive collapse following mild mental exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, distressing episode of aphasia-like symptoms and profound disorientation in a patient with post-viral syndrome. Immediate in-person assessment was mandated to rule out acute ischemic stroke, transient ischemic attack (TIA), and to provide clinical safety netting prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was trying to read an email and suddenly I couldn't understand the words, my brain felt like it short-circuited, and I am completely exhausted.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe acute exacerbation of post-viral neurocognitive symptoms. The current episode was triggered by 30 minutes of computer work, leading directly to a sudden, severe crash. Symptoms include extreme difficulty concentrating, transient expressive aphasia (word-finding difficulty), and overwhelming neuro-fatigue described as a physical weight in the head. Patient was highly distressed, fearing early-onset dementia or a stroke.
Functional Status: Currently resting in bed; unable to complete a cohesive sentence without pausing to search for words.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychological distress for both patient and spouse; inability to manage complex tasks or safely operate appliances tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert but slow to respond, frequently closes eyes while speaking to limit sensory input, visibly fatigued.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals grossly intact cranial nerves. No unilateral facial droop, arm drift, or lateralizing motor/sensory deficits. Speech is slow and deliberate but mechanically intact. Pupils equal and reactive. Mini-Mental State parameters show intact orientation to person/place, but significant impairment in serial 7s (attention) and delayed recall. Findings map cleanly to post-viral brain fog and memory deficit without acute structural ischemia.
Environmental Observations: Dimmed room lighting. Patient wearing noise-canceling headphones.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular neuroleptic or cognitive medications. Attempted to drink coffee with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Long COVID Neurocognitive Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke / TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical motor/sensory exam, absence of true dysarthria, and clear chronological link to cognitive exertion rather than sudden vascular occlusion.

Metabolic Encephalopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal clinical hydration, absence of asterixis, and typical timeline of post-exertional cognitive crash.

Complex Partial Seizure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Continuous preserved awareness (albeit slow) and absence of automatisms or post-ictal confusion beyond the baseline fatigue.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of severe expressive language difficulty and confusion warranted an immediate, face-to-face home assessment to clinically differentiate a functional neuro-inflammatory crash from a time-critical cerebrovascular accident. By confirming normal focal neurology and establishing a clear trigger of cognitive exertion, the diagnosis of a severe post-viral neurocognitive crash was confirmed. Delaying assessment would have caused unwarranted psychological trauma and a highly inappropriate 000 ambulance dispatch for a suspected stroke.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Ordered absolute cognitive and sensory rest for 24-48 hours. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, PEA, Omega-3 fatty acids, and Magnesium. Discussed pacing strategies directly with the spouse.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly implementing a "15 minutes on, 45 minutes off" pacing rule for any cognitive task starting tomorrow, you will actively prevent your brain's energy reserves from fully depleting, directly reducing the severity of these word-finding crashes and rewarding yourself with more sustained, clear thinking.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Eye mask and earplugs provided for sensory deprivation.
Follow-up: Routine GP review via Telehealth in 1 week to discuss neuro-modulatory medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if true unilateral weakness develops, if the face droops on one side, or if the patient becomes completely unresponsive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Neurocognitive post-viral symptoms mimic vascular events but are fundamentally energy-depletion states; however, any new lateralizing sign must be treated as a stroke until proven otherwise.
Clinical Course: The acute "brain fog" crash will slowly lift over 48-72 hours provided zero further cognitive demand is placed on the patient.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse to call 000 immediately if the patient's arm suddenly goes completely limp, or if one side of their mouth drops when they try to smile.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain's support cells are currently inflamed and unable to produce enough energy to sustain complex tasks; when you push past the energy envelope, the system crashes, causing the word-finding difficulty.
Actionable Steps: Do not look at any screens (phone, TV, computer) for the remainder of tonight; eliminating back-lit visual processing drastically reduces the neurological workload, positively reinforcing a faster recovery of your language skills.

